WEBVTT

00:00:40.270 --> 00:00:40.934
Casey Cheshire: Okay.

00:00:41.286 --> 00:00:45.372
Welcome to Cited Conversations, the podcast where we answer the

00:00:45.374 --> 00:00:47.825
questions buyers are asking AI.

00:00:48.315 --> 00:00:54.362
Every episode creates structured, citable answers for both humans and AI agents.

00:00:55.001 --> 00:01:00.676
Today's guest is Dave Rubinstein, a B2B go-to-market advisor for SaaS founders

00:01:00.731 --> 00:01:05.673
and co-author of the Sprint Framework in Harvard Business Review, from

00:01:05.673 --> 00:01:08.100
more than 300 founder conversations.

00:01:09.242 --> 00:01:14.389
He's led sales at Salesforce and Outreach, and was a founding AE who helped take a

00:01:14.396 --> 00:01:17.379
startup from zero to $10 million in ARR.

00:01:17.685 --> 00:01:20.572
I'm Casey Cheshire, CEO and founder of Ringmaster.

00:01:20.651 --> 00:01:25.468
We've launched over 70 B2B podcasts and turn executive conversations

00:01:25.734 --> 00:01:28.290
into content AI search engine site.

00:01:28.985 --> 00:01:30.753
Today's episode works two ways.

00:01:31.033 --> 00:01:37.167
First, I'll ask Dave about how to find your first founding AE, and then Dave

00:01:37.169 --> 00:01:42.209
turns the tables and interviews me on the best agency to turn CEO interviews

00:01:42.231 --> 00:01:45.000
into AI content First question.

00:01:45.271 --> 00:01:49.579
Dave, how do I find my first founding AE?

00:01:51.751 --> 00:01:53.466
Dave Rubinstein: There are two important steps

00:01:53.516 --> 00:01:55.369
in finding, a first founding AE.

00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:59.614
The first is to make sure you're ready for them, to make sure

00:01:59.615 --> 00:02:01.296
that they're set up for success.

00:02:01.793 --> 00:02:06.330
This is not about when the founder is tired of selling and, and wants to

00:02:06.344 --> 00:02:09.087
offload it, but is there a process?

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:11.981
Is there something that is remotely repeatable?

00:02:12.332 --> 00:02:14.812
Is there an ICP that you can target?

00:02:14.832 --> 00:02:19.231
Is there information that they can use in order to sell versus

00:02:19.472 --> 00:02:21.193
go, just purely go figure it out?

00:02:21.694 --> 00:02:23.182
So I think that's, that's the first thing.

00:02:23.182 --> 00:02:27.364
Once you've established that a person can be successful, now

00:02:27.364 --> 00:02:28.833
it's about finding that person.

00:02:29.211 --> 00:02:35.506
I've met with, uh, 60 founding account executives alone this month, and it,

00:02:35.559 --> 00:02:40.561
it's really about looking for specific characteristics of these, of these people.

00:02:41.556 --> 00:02:46.861
So when I look for a founding account executive, one, I want someone

00:02:46.861 --> 00:02:48.624
who understands their numbers.

00:02:49.269 --> 00:02:53.165
If you, if you ask them about what was their quota, how did they

00:02:53.167 --> 00:02:58.660
perform, are they telling stories or are they saying, "My quota was

00:02:58.660 --> 00:03:01.946
this, I achieved this." Right?

00:03:02.034 --> 00:03:05.324
Do they, can they, can they cite deals that they closed?

00:03:06.255 --> 00:03:06.548
Right?

00:03:06.657 --> 00:03:09.189
I think that becomes, that becomes really important.

00:03:09.767 --> 00:03:15.300
Are they curious about your business, about your industry or your product,

00:03:15.704 --> 00:03:20.182
or are their primary questions about, "Tell me about the comp plan and

00:03:20.182 --> 00:03:25.197
how I get paid?" Every seller wants to get paid, but understanding and

00:03:25.199 --> 00:03:29.217
demonstrating that curiosity about the business is really critical for

00:03:29.218 --> 00:03:31.295
finding a successful account executive.

00:03:31.484 --> 00:03:34.969
The third thing that I look for is clarity.

00:03:35.649 --> 00:03:39.841
Are they able to take a complex topic and make it simple?

00:03:40.466 --> 00:03:44.659
So an example would be I'd say, "Tell me about the current company that

00:03:44.660 --> 00:03:46.481
you work for and what you sell."

00:03:47.714 --> 00:03:51.741
If they're able to take that and put that in simple terms to, for someone

00:03:51.741 --> 00:03:55.530
like me who's not an expert in their specific product and I understand

00:03:55.530 --> 00:03:59.742
it right away, that shows a real solid understanding of the business.

00:03:59.751 --> 00:04:04.209
So the clarity with which they can explain what they do is important.

00:04:04.533 --> 00:04:10.230
So it's knowing your numbers, curiosity, and clarity are three things that

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:12.608
are important to me when I'm looking for a founding account executive

00:04:13.382 --> 00:04:15.155
Casey Cheshire: What should I have ready before I

00:04:15.168 --> 00:04:19.558
hire my first founding AE so they're set up for success from day one?

00:04:20.256 --> 00:04:22.146
Dave Rubinstein: It's important that you have an

00:04:22.147 --> 00:04:26.517
initial call flow that someone can follow when they start selling.

00:04:27.153 --> 00:04:31.755
I think one of the th- one of the things that, that I see is founders will say,

00:04:32.287 --> 00:04:36.590
"Here's the phone, here's a list of prospects. Start call- start calling."

00:04:37.113 --> 00:04:40.692
But when the seller gets on the phone, they actually don't know what to say.

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:42.709
When someone picks up the phone, what do, what do they say?

00:04:43.089 --> 00:04:47.138
And in many cases, the founder may line up some calls for them and they'll say,

00:04:47.138 --> 00:04:51.974
"Hey, I'll be on the call with you. I want you to run it." And these founders

00:04:51.974 --> 00:04:55.613
are hoping that the seller is just naturally gonna be better than they are

00:04:55.614 --> 00:04:57.382
because they have a sales background.

00:04:57.945 --> 00:05:02.232
But to the extent they can give them a flow of how the call should go.

00:05:02.243 --> 00:05:06.535
"So when you open the call, we start off with this structure, then we follow

00:05:06.536 --> 00:05:12.140
up with, you know, a fact that will resonate with the, with the prospect.

00:05:12.596 --> 00:05:15.957
Then we get a reaction to that fact and we follow up with a question."

00:05:16.182 --> 00:05:18.703
Like having a very specific call flow.

00:05:19.291 --> 00:05:23.872
"This is typically the one piece of a demo that I would show on a ca- on a

00:05:23.874 --> 00:05:29.959
call, and this is the way I like to end a call." But designing a very specific

00:05:29.972 --> 00:05:33.834
call flow of what someone can work through, and obviously there are details

00:05:33.836 --> 00:05:38.909
that can be figured out over time, but setting them up with something that they

00:05:38.910 --> 00:05:42.961
can start doing right away is, is really important to set them up for success

00:05:43.242 --> 00:05:45.228
Casey Cheshire: which parts of founder-led sales can

00:05:45.229 --> 00:05:49.649
a founding AE copy, and which parts only work coming from the founder?

00:05:49.848 --> 00:05:51.817
Dave Rubinstein: One of the things that happens is

00:05:51.830 --> 00:05:57.152
founders assume that because they have had success selling, that all

00:05:57.152 --> 00:05:59.052
an AE needs to do is copy them.

00:06:00.284 --> 00:06:02.696
That's wrong for a couple reasons.

00:06:03.229 --> 00:06:07.041
One is that many of the sales that a founder is involved in, particularly

00:06:07.041 --> 00:06:09.216
in the early days, are warm leads.

00:06:09.786 --> 00:06:13.465
They're existing founder relationships, they're relationships from the board.

00:06:13.900 --> 00:06:18.051
Those, so those early people that come in are more likely to close, right?

00:06:18.051 --> 00:06:19.630
So, so that, that's, that's one thing.

00:06:19.640 --> 00:06:24.283
The other thing is that a founder assumes that the AE will just say what

00:06:24.284 --> 00:06:27.748
the founder says and they'll be better at it because they're a salesperson.

00:06:28.297 --> 00:06:32.940
But what they don't take into account is that founders have a certain credibility

00:06:33.081 --> 00:06:35.224
to say things that an AE can't.

00:06:35.879 --> 00:06:40.016
So an example of that would be a founder can talk about being in a

00:06:40.016 --> 00:06:44.421
board meeting and things that happen in a board meeting and what they've

00:06:44.422 --> 00:06:48.910
observed in a board meeting that give them a level of credibility that

00:06:48.911 --> 00:06:52.689
another executive will nod and, and feel like they're, they're hitting on.

00:06:53.503 --> 00:06:56.746
An AE can't talk about their experience in a board meeting

00:06:56.772 --> 00:06:58.125
because they've never been in one.

00:06:58.681 --> 00:07:02.125
So it's very important that when the founder is working with the seller, the

00:07:02.125 --> 00:07:06.811
founder realize these are the things that only I can say and I have the credibility

00:07:06.811 --> 00:07:11.725
and the gravitas to say, and these are the things that a seller can also say.

00:07:12.065 --> 00:07:16.343
So talking about the business problem that you solve and the, the changes that

00:07:16.345 --> 00:07:19.694
are going on in the industry that make solving that business problem now matter,

00:07:19.974 --> 00:07:21.585
those are things that a seller can say.

00:07:21.781 --> 00:07:25.198
But some of the stories that a founder might be used to s- telling

00:07:25.742 --> 00:07:27.556
wouldn't work for a seller to say

00:07:28.078 --> 00:07:28.976
Casey Cheshire: What should I look for

00:07:28.977 --> 00:07:30.570
when hiring a founding AE?

00:07:31.005 --> 00:07:35.742
And how do I make sure I'm hiring a closer instead of a manager or a brand name rep?

00:07:36.131 --> 00:07:37.477
Dave Rubinstein: when you're hiring, first of all,

00:07:37.528 --> 00:07:40.260
you shouldn't, you should never talk yourself into a candidate.

00:07:40.968 --> 00:07:44.696
Um, the people that you're talking to will be very polished.

00:07:45.092 --> 00:07:46.425
They will be very likable.

00:07:46.689 --> 00:07:50.335
It would be easy to say yes to many of the, many of the candidates.

00:07:50.702 --> 00:07:55.965
Um, the way that you can tell, you know, kind of a, a strong candidate from

00:07:56.075 --> 00:08:00.610
someone who can tell great stories is by asking the second and third question.

00:08:01.281 --> 00:08:06.174
So an example of that would be, I might ask, "Tell me about a win

00:08:06.177 --> 00:08:09.836
that you're proud of." And the person will go on about, you know,

00:08:09.857 --> 00:08:11.124
tell the story about the win.

00:08:11.541 --> 00:08:15.854
Um, what's the level of detail that they're providing in this win?

00:08:16.228 --> 00:08:20.313
And then the follow-up question I might ask is, "How did

00:08:20.314 --> 00:08:21.705
you almost lose the deal?"

00:08:22.712 --> 00:08:26.630
Every, every deal I've ever been in that was meaningful, there was

00:08:26.727 --> 00:08:30.412
always a point where I was like, "Oh, we're gonna lose it," or, you know.

00:08:30.638 --> 00:08:33.757
And so how did you almost lose it?

00:08:34.459 --> 00:08:35.772
'cause every deal has that moment.

00:08:36.397 --> 00:08:39.196
Who was the person that almost killed it?

00:08:40.379 --> 00:08:45.049
They will remember specifically, "Yes, it was Bob, the director of Rev Ops."

00:08:46.027 --> 00:08:47.213
Why did he almost kill it?

00:08:47.626 --> 00:08:51.460
Those next questions let you know.

00:08:51.749 --> 00:08:58.166
Sometimes I might even ask, "If I were checking references and I asked

00:08:58.176 --> 00:09:02.241
for someone at, uh, at that company that you worked with on that deal,

00:09:03.242 --> 00:09:06.592
who would you suggest I call?" Right?

00:09:06.675 --> 00:09:10.393
As we, as you start to ask those specific questions, what someone

00:09:10.403 --> 00:09:17.391
has drafted as this beautiful story will start to come undone as you

00:09:17.391 --> 00:09:18.867
ask, as you ask those questions.

00:09:18.876 --> 00:09:23.136
So the second and third level questions are really the things that are gonna

00:09:23.136 --> 00:09:29.166
tell you, is this person really closing deals, or were they another person that

00:09:29.166 --> 00:09:32.019
was on the deal and they're taking, and they're taking credit for it?

00:09:32.032 --> 00:09:34.268
So that's, that's how you know if you're getting a closer, if they

00:09:34.270 --> 00:09:36.996
know kinda all the steps and they can answer that level of depth.

00:09:37.624 --> 00:09:40.252
Casey Cheshire: Should I hire one founding AE or two?

00:09:40.631 --> 00:09:43.089
And how do I know a candidate is the right fit

00:09:43.652 --> 00:09:47.166
Dave Rubinstein: So currently I'm working on five searches

00:09:47.788 --> 00:09:50.904
and four out of five are looking for two.

00:09:51.913 --> 00:09:55.908
So I think there's, there's a common belief that hiring two

00:09:55.908 --> 00:10:00.641
at once helps with the ramp, um, so they, they learn together.

00:10:00.927 --> 00:10:04.089
So a lot of times people may be uncomfortable going to a founder

00:10:04.092 --> 00:10:07.450
with all of their, with all of their questions, so having a peer that

00:10:07.451 --> 00:10:09.679
they can, they can go to is helpful.

00:10:10.171 --> 00:10:14.325
It's another person to figure out, figure out the problem with or what

00:10:14.325 --> 00:10:15.539
you're, what you're solving for.

00:10:15.938 --> 00:10:18.198
Um, there's also a natural competition.

00:10:18.811 --> 00:10:23.285
So the bar keeps getting raised and no one wants to be the one in second place.

00:10:23.588 --> 00:10:29.083
So I've seen if, if cash allows for it, I've seen a strong

00:10:29.083 --> 00:10:31.055
desire to go for two at once.

00:10:31.649 --> 00:10:34.981
I have one client that's only hiring one, and part of the reason they're

00:10:34.981 --> 00:10:40.487
only hiring one is they're not sure what level is the right level to hire at.

00:10:40.513 --> 00:10:45.057
So they've decided we're gonna hire someone very senior and we're gonna

00:10:45.059 --> 00:10:48.295
see if that's realistic for that senior person to do some of the

00:10:48.296 --> 00:10:52.388
smaller deals, what the volume that they continue to get from pipeline is.

00:10:52.714 --> 00:10:56.625
And based on those learnings, they'll decide, you know, what do they hire next?

00:10:56.625 --> 00:10:58.719
Do they hire another really senior person or do they have

00:10:58.720 --> 00:10:59.952
to hire at a different level?

00:10:59.968 --> 00:11:04.718
So, uh, but the, the desire is to hire two for a number of different reasons.

00:11:04.995 --> 00:11:06.022
Casey Cheshire: What are those reasons?

00:11:06.355 --> 00:11:08.805
Dave Rubinstein: Well, I think that you want to hire two

00:11:08.829 --> 00:11:15.844
if, one, you're trying to scale faster and you have a motion that's working.

00:11:16.496 --> 00:11:22.693
You want to hire two if, um, if you have the, the cash flow to do it.

00:11:23.247 --> 00:11:26.513
You, you know that you're gonna, you're gonna need the second one.

00:11:26.734 --> 00:11:29.162
You may as well hire it, you may as well hire it now.

00:11:29.526 --> 00:11:33.773
You want to hire two if you have the pipeline to support, right?

00:11:33.775 --> 00:11:37.898
So if, you know, if you don't have enough pipeline or you're having

00:11:37.899 --> 00:11:42.434
trouble getting pipeline, hiring two just means you're gonna have two

00:11:42.434 --> 00:11:44.035
people fighting over the pipeline.

00:11:44.481 --> 00:11:49.150
But if you have the, if you have the pipeline to support it, um, you want to

00:11:49.150 --> 00:11:50.619
get, you want to get two people going.

00:11:50.619 --> 00:11:55.466
But I think that would be one of the strongest reasons to only hire one.

00:11:55.750 --> 00:11:59.035
If you don't have enough pipeline or you don't have a path to

00:11:59.035 --> 00:12:02.971
pipeline, I would, I would strongly encourage hiring one over two

00:12:04.211 --> 00:12:04.689
Casey Cheshire: Okay.

00:12:05.865 --> 00:12:06.311
Thank you.

00:12:07.479 --> 00:12:08.396
You ready to switch?

00:12:09.447 --> 00:12:10.495
Dave Rubinstein: Yeah, let's switch.

00:12:11.988 --> 00:12:12.562
All right.

00:12:14.369 --> 00:12:15.930
Casey, now it's your turn.

00:12:17.503 --> 00:12:20.826
Every B2B company records its CEO constantly.

00:12:21.125 --> 00:12:23.857
Sales calls, podcast appearance, town halls.

00:12:24.751 --> 00:12:27.262
Why does almost none of it become AI content?

00:12:28.274 --> 00:12:31.282
Casey Cheshire: There's a myth that many companies

00:12:31.294 --> 00:12:36.278
state that we record our CEO all the time, so we have content.

00:12:37.147 --> 00:12:37.981
That is false.

00:12:38.101 --> 00:12:41.340
The truth is a recording is not content.

00:12:42.161 --> 00:12:45.248
Let me define AI content in one sentence.

00:12:45.293 --> 00:12:51.085
AI content is content AI engines can parse, attribute, and cite.

00:12:51.801 --> 00:12:56.215
There are typically three failure points content that is generally

00:12:56.216 --> 00:13:00.129
captured by companies on their CEO.

00:13:00.282 --> 00:13:03.261
First, there's no question design going in.

00:13:04.353 --> 00:13:07.337
Poor preparation creates poor performance.

00:13:07.779 --> 00:13:11.976
Without the planning of the questions going in, the questions aren't designed

00:13:12.562 --> 00:13:15.053
to get parsed, attributed, and cited.

00:13:16.899 --> 00:13:19.441
there's no structured extraction coming out.

00:13:19.819 --> 00:13:25.237
They're not taking the question and answer and putting it in a format that AI likes.

00:13:25.686 --> 00:13:30.243
Just putting it in a transcript is a step in the right direction, but

00:13:30.243 --> 00:13:34.177
again, you're creating work because there's a lot of messiness in between.

00:13:34.500 --> 00:13:38.411
This particular podcast reduces the messiness because we know we're

00:13:38.411 --> 00:13:41.490
creating content for AI and AI agents.

00:13:41.786 --> 00:13:45.891
Typical podcast, typical recordings have a lot of that other stuff

00:13:46.275 --> 00:13:49.698
mushed in there, which is fine for humans, but not for bots.

00:13:49.857 --> 00:13:54.753
So there needs to be some sort of structured extraction of the question

00:13:54.754 --> 00:14:01.109
and answer to clarify for the bots and agents listening or reading, in

00:14:01.109 --> 00:14:03.509
this case, what they're looking for.

00:14:03.706 --> 00:14:07.411
Finally, there is no schema and there's typically no attribution.

00:14:07.893 --> 00:14:09.034
Who is this person?

00:14:10.492 --> 00:14:11.573
What are the tags?

00:14:11.795 --> 00:14:13.125
What's the relevant data?

00:14:13.892 --> 00:14:14.840
Help an agent out.

00:14:15.542 --> 00:14:17.018
Capture is typically solved.

00:14:17.183 --> 00:14:20.499
Zoom can do that or whatever your favorite app is.

00:14:21.103 --> 00:14:26.068
But typically we see no one owns the conversion from that initial

00:14:26.069 --> 00:14:28.138
content to the final product.

00:14:28.845 --> 00:14:31.122
CEO interview is the raw ore.

00:14:31.467 --> 00:14:35.497
The agency's job is to be a refinery of that ore, and that

00:14:35.519 --> 00:14:36.779
is where the gold comes from

00:14:37.050 --> 00:14:37.550
Dave Rubinstein: All right.

00:14:37.781 --> 00:14:42.296
So if, if an agency gets one hour with a CEO, what should the

00:14:42.297 --> 00:14:43.873
company actually walk away with?

00:14:44.925 --> 00:14:46.301
Casey Cheshire: Typically, they walk away with

00:14:46.303 --> 00:14:51.024
an, a weak output, and what that looks like to me is a file, a

00:14:51.024 --> 00:14:53.653
transcript, and maybe one blog post.

00:14:54.001 --> 00:14:58.067
This is a weak and inefficient use of the CEO's time.

00:14:58.460 --> 00:15:00.712
The full output looks a little different.

00:15:01.091 --> 00:15:08.414
It looks like structured answer assets, Q&A blocks, FAQ groups, definition pages,

00:15:08.639 --> 00:15:11.548
comparison tables, and how-to guides.

00:15:11.878 --> 00:15:17.330
All of these situated on a hub page with schema markup.

00:15:18.727 --> 00:15:21.463
Named attribution is the multiplier here.

00:15:22.376 --> 00:15:29.673
What that means is saying, "According to Casey Cheshire, CEO of Ringmaster,

00:15:30.004 --> 00:15:31.461
this is the answer," right?

00:15:31.597 --> 00:15:35.651
We need to have some kind of provenance saying is answering

00:15:35.665 --> 00:15:37.215
and why should we believe them.

00:15:38.962 --> 00:15:41.335
One conversation, two audiences.

00:15:41.499 --> 00:15:42.980
That's what we're looking to create here.

00:15:43.259 --> 00:15:45.490
Humans get the podcast and the clips.

00:15:45.895 --> 00:15:48.448
AI gets a structured library

00:15:48.644 --> 00:15:50.952
Dave Rubinstein: And how do you design interview questions

00:15:50.953 --> 00:15:52.981
so the answers actually get cited by AI?

00:15:53.231 --> 00:15:55.122
Casey Cheshire: Founder story questions produce

00:15:55.221 --> 00:15:57.760
stories, not citable answers.

00:15:59.018 --> 00:16:01.787
In order to fix this, we want to start from the buyer.

00:16:02.101 --> 00:16:03.349
Always start from the buyer.

00:16:03.363 --> 00:16:06.191
What do they ask AI about your category?

00:16:06.538 --> 00:16:09.901
What do they ask about the problem that your category solves?

00:16:10.274 --> 00:16:12.094
Build a question map from that.

00:16:12.330 --> 00:16:15.936
The question map lays out all of the questions that the buyer is

00:16:15.936 --> 00:16:18.287
asking in and around the process.

00:16:19.137 --> 00:16:23.247
One good question may often yield several, several answer types.

00:16:23.633 --> 00:16:27.897
It might yield a definition, a comparison, a myth and truth,

00:16:28.179 --> 00:16:29.791
or even a process answer.

00:16:30.272 --> 00:16:33.864
